# Line endings, pinned for the files where they are load-bearing.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: this repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true,
# which checks files out with CRLF endings. That is harmless for source and
# docs -- the compiler does not care -- and it is fatal for anything Linux
# EXECUTES. A shell script with CRLF endings fails as:
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# ...which reads like a missing shell rather than a missing byte, and is
# exactly the kind of half-hour this repo has already spent too many of.
#
# A plain Linux `git clone` gets LF today whatever this file says. The case
# these lines defend is the other route: cloning on Windows and copying the
# file up with scp, which carries the CRLF straight to the server. Same trap
# as the three exes -- the copy you reach for is not the copy you tested.
#
# eol=lf means "this file is LF on disk EVERYWHERE, including on Windows".

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.example   text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf
Caddyfile*  text eol=lf

# Windows-side scripts are the mirror image: cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Binary assets git must never "fix".
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.wasm      binary
*.keystore  binary
*.jks       binary
